# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for COAST. See SANITIZERS.md.
#
# Run with:  TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=tests/sanitizer_suppressions/tsan.supp" ctest ...
#
# These are KNOWN false positives in third-party code that TSan cannot model.
# COAST's own synchronization (atomics, std::mutex, the sim/GUI thread seam) is
# NOT suppressed — judge COAST races by provenance, then fix them.

# --- oneTBB task system ---------------------------------------------------------
# TBB's affinity_partitioner writes per-task affinity HINTS with intentional
# relaxed races (a work-stealing scheduling optimization, not correctness state),
# and its work-stealing queues use custom synchronization TSan does not model.
# Surfaced by any non-empty parallel_for (e.g. env::dispatch_cpu_entities).
#
# Scoped by library because a precise race:<func>/<file> scope does NOT work: TSan
# inlines the TBB partitioner frames (note_affinity, partitioner.h) into the
# caller, so the only matchable frame is the libtbb.so one.
#
# CAVEAT: `called_from_lib:libtbb.so` is broad — it ALSO masks first-party races
# inside parallel_for lambda BODIES (e.g. the hd_ngm2 per-neuron loops). That is
# acceptable for the TSan test run, which is unit-tests-only (no first-party
# compute lambdas; A-tier integration is excluded under sanitizers — SANITIZERS.md).
# When AUDITING first-party parallel_for code (the hd_ngm2 partition-disjointness
# review), run TSan WITHOUT this suppression so those frames stay visible.
called_from_lib:libtbb.so
